First of all, I want to thank you for such a great plugin!

In our product we use [FSD methology](https://feature-sliced.design/) and it is crucial to us to keep track of boundaries violations.

But we discovered that boundaries can be broken not only by import statements. There are several other ways of introducing dependencies between modules:

- Re-exports. This problem is addressed in the #213
- Dynamic imports: `const Module = import('path/to/module');`
- Module mocks, for example: [jest.mock](https://jestjs.io/docs/jest-object#jestmockmodulename-factory-options)

It would be very useful to allow users define their own dependency nodes.

I came up with the following solution:

1. Introduce the new setting `boundaries/additional-dependency-nodes`:

```js
{
    // Format: [{ selector: '{esquery selector}', kind: '{value|type}' }]
    'boundaries/additional-dependency-nodes': [
        // Dynamic imports: import('source')
        { selector: 'ImportExpression > Literal', kind: 'value' },
        
        // Named exports: export { ModuleA } from 'modules/a'
        // Note ':not' pseudo-class here; it addresses parsers that do not have the exportKind property.
        { selector: 'ExportNamedDeclaration:not([exportKind=type]) > Literal', kind: 'value' },
        
        // Named type exports: export type { ModuleA } from 'modules/a'
        { selector: 'ExportNamedDeclaration[exportKind=type] > Literal', kind: 'type' },
        
        // And so on. We were able to describe all the cases we needed with this syntax.
    ]
}
```

2. Directly select `Literal` nodes to extract dependency source. This allows us to get rid of `dependencyLocation` helper and also simplifies how dependecy nodes are defined: we only need a single selector.

What do you think? Am I missing something?

If the solution is OK, I will finalize it by adding tests, validating the settings and updating the documentation.

closes #213

Websocket communication w/ the sync server should send Update objects to the server, and the server will in turn send Update
objects to the client. Client should only update the canvas or palette when the update from the server has a newer timestsamp.

When the client first connects, the server will send an Update object encompassing the entirety of the current canvas and palette.

When populating the timestamp field, the server expects a Java long integer in milliseconds (basically `new Date().getTIme()` rounded to nearest integer)

```typescript
interface Pixel {
    colorIndex: number,
    timestamp: number,
    x: number,
    y: number
}

interface Palette {
    colors: string[],
    timestamp: number
}

interface Update {
    pixels?: Pixel[],
    palette?: Palette
}
```

